.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":1310000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":19032,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":22401,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2601.42,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":201.01,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[{"title":"Annual governance statement 2026","url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s38553/annual-governance-statement-2026.pdf","committee":"Audit & Governance","date":"14/07/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Audit & Governance","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-12","committee":"Community & Resources","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-20","committee":"Internal Overview & Scrutiny","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"External Overview & Scrutiny","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-23","committee":"Joint Planning Policy Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-16","committee":"Community & Resources","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Internal Overview & Scrutiny","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-27","committee":"Full Council","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Internal Overview & Scrutiny","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Community & Resources","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"External Overview & Scrutiny","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Torridge saves \u00a321.93 per household through treasury management outperformance","lead":"Torridge council delivered net interest income of \u00a321.93 per household in 2025-26, swinging from a budgeted cost of \u00a339.43 per property as investment returns grew and borrowing remained minimal. The council took in \u00a31.43 million from short-term investments\u201472 per cent more than the previous year\u2014by deploying additional cash into investments despite falling interest rates. This was achieved while managing capital spending of \u00a319.6 million without any new external borrowing, instead relying on grants, revenue contributions and its substantial cash reserves. The council holds only \u00a3191,000 of Public Works Loan Board debt (the government's lending arm for councils) and has built liquid reserves to \u00a328 million, a 40 per cent increase year-on-year. Treasury staff noted, however, that further interest rate cuts by the Bank of England toward its 2 per cent inflation target could pressure investment returns in future.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"12 - Annual Report on Treasury Management 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40144/40144.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"12a - Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40145/40145.pdf","headline":"Short-term borrowing maturity at 55% exceeds prudential target of 30%, though council explains this reflects its small scale","detail":"The maturity structure shows 55% of fixed-rate borrowing due within 12 months, 25 percentage points above the 30% upper target. The council's outstanding PWLB borrowing is only \u00a3191k, with other borrowing reflecting IFRS 16 lease liabilities. The council notes this deviation is structural\u2014small authorities with few loans find it difficult to meet the maturity targets designed for larger authorities.","category":"governance","matters":"Maturity structure is a key prudential indicator. Concentration of repayments in the short term creates refinancing risk, though the council's explanation suggests this is a composition issue rather than a borrowing risk problem.","figures":"Under 12 months 55% actual vs 30% upper target; \u00a3191k PWLB borrowing outstanding","quote":"Torridge currently only has \u00a3191k outstanding in loans due to PWLB in 2025/26. Other borrowing relates to the recognitions of future Right of Use lease liabilities recognised under IFRS 16.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"12a - Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40145/40145.pdf","headline":"Financing costs achieved net interest income benefit of \u00a321.93 per Band D property against estimated cost of \u00a339.43","detail":"The ratio of financing costs to net revenue stream was -14.69% actual (2025-26), meaning interest income exceeded borrowing costs. This contrasts sharply with -1.11% estimate. The council's treasury operations delivered a net saving of \u00a321.93 per Band D property across 26,408 properties, a \u00a361 per property swing from the budgeted cost position.","category":"income","matters":"The scale of variance (\u00a339.43 cost estimated becoming \u00a321.93 saving actual) indicates either significant treasury outperformance or material changes in interest rate environment that affected the council's net interest position. Both matter to finance directors and those assessing financial management quality.","figures":"Financing costs -\u00a31,744,000; ratio -14.69% actual vs -1.11% estimate; per Band D property -\u00a321.93 actual vs \u00a339.43 estimate; 26,408 Band D equivalent properties","quote":"The impact of the decrease in net financing costs when spread across the 26,408 band D equivalent properties in the Borough equates to \u00a321.93 per property per annum.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"12a - Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40145/40145.pdf","headline":"No additional external borrowing undertaken in 2025-26 despite capital spending rising to \u00a319.6m and CFR reaching \u00a33.9m","detail":"The council managed capital spending of \u00a319.6m and a capital financing requirement of \u00a33.9m without new external borrowing, instead using existing debt capacity, grants (\u00a311.3m), revenue contributions (\u00a35.7m), and internal resources (\u00a32.2m internal borrowing). This contrasts with the estimate which assumed \u00a35.4m of internal borrowing.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Borrowing decisions matter to lenders. A council choosing not to seek new external financing when CFR is materially present signals confidence in existing cash position and reduced future refinancing requirements.","figures":"No new external borrowing; total capital spending \u00a319,589k; CFR \u00a33,901k; internal borrowing used \u00a32,152k against estimate of \u00a35,417k","quote":"No additional external borrowing was undertaken during 2025/26. Actual borrowing for the year as shown above, is significantly below the Authorised limit.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"12a - Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Outturn 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40145/40145.pdf","headline":"Capital programme underspent by 50% at \u00a319.6m against \u00a339m budget, with delays to Town Hall project","detail":"Actual capital expenditure was \u00a319.6m compared to estimate of \u00a338.9m. The council attributes this to delays in capital projects, particularly the Town Hall. This represents material slippage in capital programme delivery affecting both timing and scope of planned works.","category":"capital","matters":"Lenders assess capital programme delivery as an indicator of execution capability and financial control. A 50% variance signals project delivery risk or scope changes that affect council finances and debt servicing.","figures":"Actual \u00a319,589k; Estimate \u00a338,978k; variance \u00a319,389k (50%)","quote":"The variance between actual and estimated capital expenditure is due to the delay in spend on capital projects such as the Town Hall.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"12 - Annual Report on Treasury Management 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40144/40144.pdf","headline":"Future investment income threatened by expected further Bank of England rate cuts toward 2% inflation target","detail":"The council explicitly states that further reductions in the base rate may pressure investment returns. Rates have been static at 3.75% since December 2025, but with the BoE targeting 2% inflation, the trajectory points downward. The \u00a31.4m investment income achieved this year assumes current or higher rates and may not be sustainable.","category":"income","matters":"A finance director must budget for scenario where this year's \u00a31.85m total interest income declines; a lender needs to understand whether the council's financial plan remains stable if investment yield falls materially.","figures":"Base rate 3.75% (static since December 2025); BoE inflation target 2%","quote":"Further reductions in the bank rate may place pressure on investment returns in future given the Bank of England's 2% inflation target.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-21"},"name":"Torridge","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":false,"headline":"Short-term borrowing maturity at 55% exceeds prudential target of 30%, though council explains this reflects its small scale","lead":"Maturity structure is a key prudential indicator. Concentration of repayments in the short term creates refinancing risk, though the council's explanation suggests this is a composition issue rather than a borrowing risk problem.","also":["8 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Full Council on 2026-09-21","n_findings":9,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"12a - Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Outturn 2025-26","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.torridge.gov.uk/documents/s40145/40145.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":55,"news_drafts_total":1}}